<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vietnamese pre-trained encoders have improved rapidly, but the
strongest ones are large, multilingual-vocabulary models or depend on
external word segmentation, which makes them costly to deploy.
We present <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GoiBERT</span>, a family of two compact Vietnamese
encoders with 26M and 49M parameters that operate directly on raw text.
GoiBERT learns from two complementary teachers.
A same-tokenizer Vietnamese encoder first teaches general-purpose
representations.
A much larger teacher then provides task-level guidance through soft
labels that are generated without ever labeling its own training data.
Across eight benchmarks covering text classification, aspect-based
sentiment analysis, natural language inference, and named entity
recognition, GoiBERT-large achieves the highest average score among
non-teacher models.
It does so with fewer than half the parameters of the strongest compact
baseline and twice its throughput.
GoiBERT-base retains 99% of that baseline’s performance at a quarter
of its size.
Our analysis shows that compression is nearly lossless for sentiment
and classification tasks but remains costly for inference and entity
recognition, which points to clear directions for future work. Our code and models are available at: <a href="https://github.com/Nhantran65/GoiBERT.git" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/Nhantran65/GoiBERT.git</a></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pre-trained Transformer encoders remain the workhorse of practical language
understanding: they power text classification, sentiment analysis,
information extraction, and retrieval in production systems where
generative large language models are too slow or too costly to serve
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2019b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conneau et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warner et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Vietnamese NLP has followed this trajectory closely, moving from
multilingual encoders to a growing family of monolingual models such as
PhoBERT, ViDeBERTa, ViSoBERT, and the recent BamiBERT, as well as
CafeBERT, a large XLM-R model further adapted to Vietnamese
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tuan Nguyen (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2023b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Do et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These models have steadily raised the bar on Vietnamese benchmarks, but
each comes with a deployment cost.
The strongest model, CafeBERT, has 559M parameters, and more than
45% of them sit in its multilingual embedding matrix.
PhoBERT expects word-segmented input and therefore needs an external
segmenter at inference time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vu et al. (2018)</a></cite>.
ViSoBERT targets social-media text, and even the most compact strong
monolingual baseline, BamiBERT, uses a 102M-parameter encoder.
For the many Vietnamese applications that run on CPUs, small GPUs, or
under tight latency budgets, the field still lacks a <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compact</em>,
<em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">segmentation-free</em> encoder that holds up across diverse tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30067v1/teaser.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/329;" width="476" height="329"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Average score over eight Vietnamese benchmarks versus model
size. Both GoiBERT variants lie on the accuracy–efficiency frontier
of non-teacher models: GoiBERT-large has the highest average with
less than half of BamiBERT’s parameters, and GoiBERT-base stays close
with about a quarter of them.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge distillation (KD) is the standard tool for closing this gap in
English, where compact students such as DistilBERT, TinyBERT, MobileBERT,
and MiniLM retain most of their teachers’ accuracy at a fraction of their
cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanh et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
Transferring these recipes to Vietnamese, however, raises a design
question that English work rarely faces: <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which teacher should the
student learn from?</em>
The most accurate Vietnamese teacher (CafeBERT) uses a large
multilingual vocabulary that does not match any compact Vietnamese
tokenizer.
This rules out the token-level hidden-state and attention matching
that makes TinyBERT-style general distillation effective.
A same-tokenizer teacher (BamiBERT) supports such matching but is
itself weaker on several tasks.
To our knowledge, no prior work has studied how to combine these
complementary signals for Vietnamese, and Vietnamese compact encoders
have not been evaluated systematically for both accuracy and deployment
cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We present <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GoiBERT</span>, a family of two compact Vietnamese encoders:
GoiBERT-base (26.4M parameters) and GoiBERT-large (49.4M parameters).
Our key idea is to <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">assign each teacher to the stage where its
strengths matter</em>.
In the first stage, BamiBERT, which shares GoiBERT’s tokenizer, teaches
general-purpose representations through layer-wise hidden-state and
attention transfer on unlabeled text.
In the second stage, CafeBERT supplies task-specific soft labels at the
output level, where tokenizer mismatch is irrelevant.
We produce these labels <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">out of fold</em>, so that no teacher ever
labels an example it was trained on.
As Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows, the resulting students move the
accuracy–efficiency frontier for Vietnamese.
GoiBERT-large achieves the best average score among non-teacher models
with fewer than half of BamiBERT’s parameters and twice its GPU
throughput.
GoiBERT-base retains 99% of BamiBERT’s average score with a quarter of
its parameters and 3.5<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> its throughput.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span> We release GoiBERT-base and GoiBERT-large,
compact Vietnamese encoders that process raw text without word
segmentation and offer two complementary accuracy–efficiency
operating points.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:1.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method.</span> We propose a two-teacher, two-stage
distillation framework. It pairs representation transfer from a
tokenizer-compatible teacher with out-of-fold, output-level
supervision from a stronger cross-tokenizer teacher, and it combines
this with teacher-derived embedding initialization and layer-wise
learning-rate decay.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:1.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis.</span> We evaluate on eight benchmarks (15 metrics,
three seeds) and measure parameters, FLOPs, throughput, memory, and
CPU latency. Ablations and per-task analysis show where compression
is nearly free (sentiment, ABSA, topic, and spam classification) and
where it still costs accuracy (NLI and NER).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work draws on three lines of research: Vietnamese pre-trained
encoders, knowledge distillation for compact Transformers, and
efficient language-specific models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Vietnamese pre-trained encoders.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Vietnamese NLP has moved from multilingual encoders such as XLM-R
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conneau et al. (2020)</a></cite> to a growing family of monolingual models.
After early BERT and ELECTRA adaptations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bui et al. (2020)</a></cite>,
PhoBERT set strong baselines but requires word-segmented input from an
external segmenter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tuan Nguyen (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vu et al. (2018)</a></cite>.
This design choice reflects a property of the language: Vietnamese is
written as space-separated syllables, and many words span several of
them, so a model must either receive word boundaries explicitly or learn
them from raw text.
Later models changed the pre-training objective (ViDeBERTa;
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al., 2023</a></cite>) or the target domain (ViSoBERT for social
media and ViHealthBERT for health;
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Minh et al., 2022</a></cite>).
Others added linguistic inductive bias: HuTieuBERT injects
morpheme-boundary information into a pre-trained encoder to reflect the
isolating structure of Vietnamese <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinh et al. (2026)</a></cite>.
BamiBERT is a recent general-purpose raw-text encoder
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2026)</a></cite>, while CafeBERT continues pre-training
XLM-R-large on Vietnamese and leads the VLUE benchmark
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Do et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Generative models such as ViT5, BARTpho, and PhoGPT
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Phan et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2023a)</a></cite> target text
generation and are complementary to encoders, which remain the
cost-effective choice for understanding tasks.
Benchmarks such as VLUE and ViGLUE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Do et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al. (2024)</a></cite>
have made these models comparable, but only in terms of accuracy.
Similar efforts for other medium-resource languages, such as
Indonesian, Indic languages, Bangla, and Thai, have likewise focused on
full-size models
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilie et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kakwani et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattacharjee et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lowphansirikul et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
This line of work has therefore steadily raised accuracy while model
sizes have stayed around or above 100M parameters.
GoiBERT asks the complementary question of how much of that accuracy
can be retained at a fraction of the cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Knowledge distillation for compact encoders.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge distillation (KD) trains a small student to mimic the
softened predictions of a larger teacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al. (2015)</a></cite>.
For BERT-style encoders, KD has been extended from output logits to
intermediate signals.
PKD and DistilBERT match intermediate layers or initialize the student
from the teacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2019b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanh et al. (2019)</a></cite>, MobileBERT
transfers layer-wise features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2020)</a></cite>, and MiniLM
transfers self-attention distributions
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
TinyBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2020)</a></cite> combines these signals into the
two-stage scheme we build on: general distillation of hidden states and
attention on unlabeled text, followed by task-specific distillation.
The general stage lets a compact student acquire broad language
knowledge without being pre-trained from scratch, and the task stage
then specializes it with the teacher’s predictions.
Comparative studies further show that pre-training the student
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turc et al. (2019)</a></cite> and the choice of distillation objective
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Udagawa et al. (2023)</a></cite> strongly affect the final model.
Two findings from this literature shape our design.
First, a teacher much larger than its student can transfer poorly
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho and Hariharan (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirzadeh et al. (2020)</a></cite>, which favors a mid-sized teacher
for representation transfer.
Second, soft labels partly act as a regularizer, so a student can match
or even surpass its teacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Furlanello et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
Existing two-stage methods use a single teacher in both stages, and
multi-teacher approaches combine several teachers into one supervision
signal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2019a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Avram et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
When teacher and student vocabularies differ, distillation must
additionally align them <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boizard et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
GoiBERT sidesteps this problem by giving each teacher its own stage.
A same-tokenizer teacher supervises representations, and a
cross-tokenizer teacher supervises only task outputs, whose soft labels
we generate out of fold in the spirit of stacked generalization
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wolpert (1992)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficient language-specific encoders.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A complementary line of work builds compact models for a single
language.
One route starts from multilingual models, either trimming their
vocabulary <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdaoui et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ushio et al. (2023)</a></cite> or distilling
them into language-specific students
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee and Hassan Awadallah (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ansell et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cruz (2025)</a></cite>.
Another distills monolingual teachers, as in DistilCamemBERT for French
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delestre and Amar (2022)</a></cite>.
In both cases, the student typically inherits its vocabulary and its
supervision from the same single teacher.
GoiBERT combines the two routes.
Its tokenizer and initial embeddings come from a monolingual teacher,
in line with evidence that initializing small models from larger ones
helps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samragh et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Its task supervision comes from a teacher of multilingual origin.
Pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanh et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al. (2022)</a></cite>, quantization
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and parameter sharing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan et al. (2020)</a></cite> are
orthogonal and can be applied on top of
GoiBERT.
Finally, parameter counts and FLOPs can misrepresent real speed
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
Following recent efficiency-focused encoders such as ModernBERT
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warner et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we therefore report measured throughput,
memory, and latency alongside predictive accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>GoiBERT</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.30067v1/Training_pipeline_Final.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/162;" width="381" height="162"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of the GoiBERT training pipeline.
<span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> General distillation: the student matches the hidden
states and attention maps of a frozen BamiBERT, which shares its
tokenizer, on unlabeled Vietnamese text.
<span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> Out-of-fold soft labels: five CafeBERT teachers, each
fine-tuned on four folds, label the held-out fold, so that no
example is labeled by a teacher that saw it.
<span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> Task fine-tuning: the distilled backbone is trained on
gold labels and CafeBERT soft labels with layer-wise learning-rate
decay.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GoiBERT is designed around one observation.
The two strongest available teachers offer <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">different kinds</em> of
knowledge, and each kind can only be transferred at a particular
granularity.
BamiBERT tokenizes text exactly as GoiBERT does, so its token-level
hidden states and attention maps can be matched position by position.
CafeBERT is considerably more accurate on several tasks, but its
250K-entry multilingual vocabulary segments the same sentence
differently, so its knowledge can only be transferred cleanly through
sequence- or word-level predictions.
GoiBERT therefore uses BamiBERT for <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">task-agnostic representation
transfer</em> and CafeBERT for <em id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">task-specific output supervision</em>
(Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 GoiBERT ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), extending the two-stage design of
TinyBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2020)</a></cite> to two heterogeneous teachers with complementary roles.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Compact Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GoiBERT follows the RoBERTa encoder architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2019b)</a></cite>
and reuses BamiBERT’s 20,481-token vocabulary and tokenizer, so it
processes raw Vietnamese text without word segmentation.
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GoiBERT-base</span> has 10 Transformer layers, hidden size 384,
6 attention heads, and a 1,536-dimensional feed-forward layer
(26.4M parameters).
<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GoiBERT-large</span> has 12 layers, hidden size 512, 8 heads, and a
2,048-dimensional feed-forward layer (49.4M parameters).
Both use 64-dimensional attention heads, as BamiBERT does.
The compact vocabulary is itself a source of efficiency.
Embeddings account for only 33% and 23% of the parameters of
GoiBERT-base and GoiBERT-large, respectively, compared with 46% for
CafeBERT and 69% for XLM-R (Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Layer-wise learning-rate decay. ‣ Appendix B Distillation Details ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
To inherit lexical knowledge from the teacher, we initialize
GoiBERT-large’s word embeddings by projecting BamiBERT’s 768-dimensional
embedding matrix onto its 512 leading principal components and
rescaling to the student’s initialization scale.
Transformer blocks are initialized randomly.
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Architecture and Initialization ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> gives a layer-level diagram.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Stage 1: General Distillation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the first stage, the student learns from a frozen BamiBERT on
unlabeled text by matching its intermediate representations.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\{\mathrm{base},\mathrm{large}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>base</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>large</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\{\mathrm{base},\mathrm{large}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a variant with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>
layers and hidden size <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{v}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{v}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the student’s hidden states
at layer <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding output), and let
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{B}_{\pi_{v}(\ell)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 768}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>768</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{B}_{\pi_{v}(\ell)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 768}</annotation></semantics></math> be those of the teacher
layer paired with it by the mapping <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
GoiBERT-large has 12 layers like BamiBERT, so <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{large}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>large</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{large}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the identity.
GoiBERT-base has only 10 layers, which rules out a one-to-one pairing.
Instead, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>base</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an order-preserving mapping that pairs
the embedding outputs and selects 10 of the teacher’s 12 layers as
targets, so that lower student layers learn from lower teacher layers
and upper from upper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2020)</a></cite>
(Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Distillation Details ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because the hidden sizes differ, a trainable linear projection
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{v}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{v}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> maps student states into the teacher space:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{hid}}=\frac{1}{L_{v}+1}\sum_{\ell=0}^{L_{v}}\operatorname{MSE}_{\mathrm{nonpad}}\!\bigl(P^{v}_{\ell}(H^{v}_{\ell}),\,H^{B}_{\pi_{v}(\ell)}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>hid</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>MSE</mi><mi>nonpad</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{hid}}=\frac{1}{L_{v}+1}\sum_{\ell=0}^{L_{v}}\operatorname{MSE}_{\mathrm{nonpad}}\!\bigl(P^{v}_{\ell}(H^{v}_{\ell}),\,H^{B}_{\pi_{v}(\ell)}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the error is computed only over non-padding positions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The two models also have different numbers of attention heads, so
head-to-head matching is undefined.
We instead match head-averaged attention distributions
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}^{m}_{\ell}=\frac{1}{a_{m}}\sum_{h=1}^{a_{m}}A^{m}_{\ell,h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}^{m}_{\ell}=\frac{1}{a_{m}}\sum_{h=1}^{a_{m}}A^{m}_{\ell,h}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{m}_{\ell,h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{m}_{\ell,h}</annotation></semantics></math> is the post-softmax attention matrix of head <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> in
model <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{att}}=\frac{1}{L_{v}}\sum_{\ell=1}^{L_{v}}\operatorname{MSE}\!\bigl(\bar{A}^{v}_{\ell},\,\bar{A}^{B}_{\pi_{v}(\ell)}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>att</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.376em;" width="2.376em"><mi>MSE</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{att}}=\frac{1}{L_{v}}\sum_{\ell=1}^{L_{v}}\operatorname{MSE}\!\bigl(\bar{A}^{v}_{\ell},\,\bar{A}^{B}_{\pi_{v}(\ell)}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Averaging keeps the objective architecture-agnostic while still
transferring <em id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">where</em> each layer attends
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
The general-distillation objective is</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
