<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Stage 2: Out-of-Fold Task Distillation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the second stage, the distilled backbone is fine-tuned on each
downstream task with gold labels and soft labels from CafeBERT.
A naive approach would fine-tune CafeBERT on the full training set and
then label that same set.
However, a 559M-parameter teacher tends to fit its training examples
closely, so its predictions on them become nearly one-hot and carry
little of the “dark knowledge” that makes soft labels useful
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stanton et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
We therefore adopt a cross-fitting scheme inspired by stacked
generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wolpert (1992)</a></cite>.
We split the training set into five folds, fine-tune one CafeBERT on
each set of four folds, and use it to predict only the held-out fold.
Every training example thus receives a soft target from a teacher that
has never seen it, which reflects the teacher’s genuine uncertainty on
that example.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Given gold label <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, student logits <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, teacher logits <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(T)}_{m}=\operatorname{softmax}(z_{m}/T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(T)}_{m}=\operatorname{softmax}(z_{m}/T)</annotation></semantics></math>, the task objective is</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}={}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}={}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha\,\operatorname{CE}\bigl(y,p_{S}^{(1)}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>CE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha\,\operatorname{CE}\bigl(y,p_{S}^{(1)}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+(1-\alpha)\,T^{2}\,\operatorname{KL}\bigl(p_{C}^{(T)}\,\|\,p_{S}^{(T)}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>T</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>KL</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+(1-\alpha)\,T^{2}\,\operatorname{KL}\bigl(p_{C}^{(T)}\,\|\,p_{S}^{(T)}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because the supervision is at the output level, the tokenizer mismatch
between CafeBERT and GoiBERT needs no vocabulary alignment
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boizard et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
For sequence classification and NLI, Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.3 Stage 2: Out-of-Fold Task Distillation ‣ 3 GoiBERT ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is
applied once per example.
For ABSA, it is applied independently to each aspect slot.
For NER, both models’ predictions are aligned to the first subword of
each original syllable, which yields a shared unit of supervision across both tokenizers.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layer-wise learning-rate decay.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During task fine-tuning, layer <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> of the student uses learning rate
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\ell}=\eta\,\gamma^{L_{v}-\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\ell}=\eta\,\gamma^{L_{v}-\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, the embeddings
use <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\gamma^{L_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\gamma^{L_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the task head uses <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Howard and Ruder (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2019a)</a></cite>.
Lower layers, which hold the most transferable features learned in
Stage 1, are therefore updated more conservatively than upper,
task-specific ones.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on eight Vietnamese tasks that span four task families and
several domains (Appendix Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix C Benchmark Details ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Text classification</em> covers sentiment and topic classification of
student feedback in UIT-VSFC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2018)</a></cite> and four-class
spam detection of e-commerce reviews in ViSpamReviews
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Dinh et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Aspect-based sentiment analysis</em> (ABSA) covers smartphone reviews
in UIT-ViSFD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luc Phan et al. (2021)</a></cite> and the Restaurant and Hotel domains of
UIT-ABSA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Thin et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Natural language inference</em> (NLI) uses ViNLI
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huynh et al. (2022)</a></cite> (the four-label community mirror
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">presencesw/vinli_4_label</span>).
<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Named entity recognition</em> (NER) uses PhoNER_COVID19
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Truong et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
We keep the official training, validation, and test splits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classification and NLI are scored with accuracy and macro-F1, and ABSA
with aspect-detection F1 and end-to-end aspect–sentiment pair
micro-F1. NER is scored with strict entity-level F1.
This yields 15 metrics in total (Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Complete Benchmark Results ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>).
In the main text we report one primary metric per task: macro-F1 for
classification and NLI, pair F1 for ABSA, and entity F1 for NER.
All locally trained models are averaged over three fine-tuning seeds
(42, 43, 44) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dodge et al. (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare against the monolingual encoders PhoBERT-base-v2
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tuan Nguyen (2020)</a></cite>, ViSoBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2023b)</a></cite>, and
BamiBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2026)</a></cite>; the multilingual XLM-R-base
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conneau et al. (2020)</a></cite>; and CafeBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Do et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which is also
our task teacher.
BamiBERT is our principal comparison because it is the most recent
compact Vietnamese encoder and our Stage 1 teacher.
We re-run it under the same downstream protocol as GoiBERT, with its
learning rate tuned on validation data and standard cross-entropy
fine-tuning.
ViDeBERTa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al. (2023)</a></cite> results are reported in
Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Complete Benchmark Results ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distillation corpus.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">General distillation uses 328.6M tokens of clean, unlabeled Vietnamese
text from HPLT 3.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oepen et al. (2026)</a></cite>, Wikipedia, and the
<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training</em> splits of selected benchmarks.
To prevent leakage, we remove every passage that matches validation or
test content under exact matching or 13-gram overlap
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">General distillation runs for 120K steps (batch 32, sequence length
256) with AdamW, learning rate <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and linear decay, on a single
NVIDIA L40S GPU in bfloat16.
Task fine-tuning runs for 30 epochs with batch size 32.
Learning rates and checkpoints are selected on validation data only.
Efficiency is measured on the encoder alone.
GPU throughput and peak memory are measured on an L40S (bf16, batch 32,
length 256), and CPU latency on a single 128-token input with eight
threads (fp32).
The same protocol is applied to every model.
Full hyperparameters are given in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Training and Measurement Details ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
