is the gap between GoiBERT-large and its 11<math id="S5.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> larger task teacher
CafeBERT.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Where Does Compression Hurt? ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the per-task differences from BamiBERT,
and Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Where Does Compression Hurt? ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> groups tasks by family and compares
GoiBERT-large with both of its teachers.
The task ranking is nearly identical for both variants, so the pattern
reflects the tasks rather than single-model noise.
A clear pattern emerges.
On <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">classification</em>, GoiBERT-large slightly <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">surpasses</em> its
559M-parameter task teacher on average (80.19 vs. 79.98).
It also exceeds the teacher on three individual tasks (ViSpam,
ABSA-Hotel, and VSFC-Topic).
On <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ABSA</em>, it stays within 0.6 points of the teacher.
This is consistent with prior observations that soft targets act as a
data-dependent regularizer and can allow students to match or surpass
their teachers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Furlanello et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
Out-of-fold targets may strengthen this effect, since they encode the
teacher’s uncertainty on unseen examples rather than its memorized
labels.
Excluding ViNLI, GoiBERT-large averages 81.50 over the remaining seven
tasks, only 0.44 points below CafeBERT (81.94), while being
11<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> smaller in parameter count.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The remaining gap lies almost entirely in <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">NLI</em> and, to a lesser
extent, <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">NER</em>.
Two observations suggest that the ViNLI gap reflects limited capacity
rather than a lack of supervision.
First, ViNLI is the task most sensitive to model size within the GoiBERT
family: the base–large gap is 2.66 points, compared with 0.89 on
average across the other tasks.
Second, ViNLI gains <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>1.4 (large) and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>3.0 (base) points from task
distillation (§<a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>), so the teacher’s signal is
informative, but a 12-layer, 512-dimensional
student cannot fully absorb the cross-sentence reasoning that the
24-layer, 1024-dimensional teacher performs.
For NER, two factors may contribute.
Entity F1 is already high (above 92 for all models), leaving little
room for distillation to help.
In addition, aligning supervision to the first subword of each syllable
discards the multi-syllable word boundaries that PhoBERT receives from
its segmenter, and these boundaries frequently coincide with entity
boundaries in Vietnamese.
Distillation objectives tailored to inference and span structure are a
natural next step.</p>
</div>
</section>
