<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Efficiency–Accuracy Trade-off ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> turns the parameter and FLOP reductions of
Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Results and Analysis ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> into measured deployment gains.
Compared with BamiBERT, GoiBERT-large doubles GPU throughput, halves
peak memory, and cuts CPU latency by 37%.
GoiBERT-base processes 3.5<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> as many sentences per second and
needs less than a third of the memory.
Together with Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, these results place both
variants on the accuracy–efficiency frontier.
GoiBERT-large dominates every baseline except the 11<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> larger
CafeBERT, and GoiBERT-base is the only model below 30M parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The measured speed-ups are, however, smaller than the FLOP ratios
suggest.
GoiBERT-base needs 4.7<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer FLOPs than BamiBERT but runs
only 3.5<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster on GPU and 2.2<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster on CPU.
At small widths, memory-bound operations such as layer normalization
and embedding lookups, together with fixed per-call overhead, take up a
growing share of runtime.
This echoes the warning that FLOPs and parameter counts are imperfect
proxies for real efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
It also makes GoiBERT-large, whose 2.2<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> FLOP reduction
translates almost fully into a 2.0<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> GPU speed-up, the more
balanced choice when accuracy matters.
GoiBERT-base is preferable for CPU-only or memory-constrained serving.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Where do the parameters go?</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Layer-wise learning-rate decay. ‣ Appendix B Distillation Details ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> breaks each model’s size into embedding and
encoder parameters, which explains much of the efficiency picture.
Multilingual vocabularies impose a large fixed cost:
69% of XLM-R-base and 46% of CafeBERT are embeddings, and these
parameters add memory without adding per-token computation.
BamiBERT’s compact 20K-token vocabulary already avoids most of this
cost, so GoiBERT’s savings over BamiBERT come mainly from the encoder:
37.8M encoder parameters for GoiBERT-large and 17.7M for GoiBERT-base,
compared with 85.1M.
Consequently, the encoder is where further compression should target,
for example through pruning or quantization
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2021)</a></cite>, whereas for multilingual models
vocabulary trimming is the more natural first step
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdaoui et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ushio et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Which variant should practitioners use?</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The two variants serve different deployment regimes.
When accuracy is the priority and a GPU is available, GoiBERT-large is
the better default.
It is the strongest non-teacher model on average, roughly matches
CafeBERT on classification and ABSA, and still halves BamiBERT’s
serving cost.
When the budget is tight, such as CPU-only inference, edge devices, or
high-throughput batch labeling, GoiBERT-base offers the lowest latency
and memory with an average loss of only 1.1 points relative to
GoiBERT-large.
For NLI-heavy applications, a larger encoder remains preferable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
