<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General distillation is indispensable.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training the compact architecture from scratch fails on tasks that
require compositional understanding: ViNLI macro-F1 is only 45.02 and
ABSA-Restaurant pair F1 is 59.35.
Stage 1 recovers <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>31.2 and <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>13.5 points on these tasks,
respectively, for GoiBERT-large.
In contrast, VSFC-Topic gains less than one point, since lexical cues
already go a long way for topic classification.
The magnitude of these gains confirms that representation transfer from
a tokenizer-compatible teacher is the backbone of the recipe
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turc et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiao et al. (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-tokenizer task distillation adds consistent gains.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Out-of-fold CafeBERT supervision improves five of the six settings, by
up to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>3.0 points (ViNLI, GoiBERT-base).
For GoiBERT-base, its largest effect is on ViNLI, the task with the
widest teacher–student gap.
This supports our design choice to bring in the stronger teacher at
the stage where its tokenizer does not matter.
The one decrease (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.6 on VSFC-Topic for GoiBERT-base) is small and
is more than recovered once LLRD is subsequently added.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLRD helps uniformly.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Layer-wise decay improves every task for both variants, by 0.9–1.8
points.
This is consistent with the intuition that the lower layers distilled
in Stage 1 should be preserved during fine-tuning
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Howard and Ruder (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
Overall, the full pipeline improves on cross-entropy fine-tuning of an
undistilled student by 16.7, 3.0, and 33.5 points for GoiBERT-large.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stability across seeds.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GoiBERT-large is also more stable than BamiBERT under fine-tuning.
Its seed range (max<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>min) is narrower on 11 of the 15 metrics, and
its average range is 0.61 points versus 0.85 for BamiBERT
(Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Seed-wise Variation ‣ GoiBERT: Compact Vietnamese Language Models via Two-Stage Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).
This is in line with the regularizing effect of soft targets
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al. (2019)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced GoiBERT, a family of compact, segmentation-free
Vietnamese encoders trained by two-stage distillation from two
complementary teachers.
A tokenizer-compatible teacher transfers general representations, and a
much larger cross-tokenizer teacher supplies out-of-fold task
supervision.
GoiBERT-large (49.4M parameters) achieves the best average score among
non-teacher models across eight benchmarks, doubles the GPU throughput
of BamiBERT, and comes within half a point of its 559M-parameter teacher
outside NLI.
GoiBERT-base (26.4M) retains 99% of BamiBERT’s average with a quarter
of its parameters.
Our analysis shows that compression is nearly free for classification
and ABSA but still costly for inference and entity recognition.
This two-teacher recipe should also transfer to other languages whose
strongest models are multilingual.
Future work will target NLI- and NER-specific distillation, longer
contexts, unseen domains, and quantization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our comparison with BamiBERT is not a pure architecture comparison.
GoiBERT also benefits from CafeBERT distillation, layer-wise
learning-rate decay, and a general-distillation corpus that includes
benchmark <em id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training</em> text, while our BamiBERT re-run uses plain
cross-entropy fine-tuning. We should also be upfront that we decided to build the larger variant after looking at earlier test results, so test feedback partly shaped how the models were developed.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the statistical side, we ran general distillation only once.
The seed ranges we report therefore reflect fine-tuning variability
alone, and they are not confidence intervals or significance tests.
Our ablations cover just three tasks and add components one at a time, so they do not fully separate how the components interact.
The 8- and 15-metric averages are handy summaries, but they mix metrics that mean different things.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are also things we did not test. We distill on sequences of at most 256 tokens, so we do not know how GoiBERT handles long inputs, and none of our benchmarks probe unseen domains or dialectal variation. Our efficiency numbers cover encoder inference on the hardware we used. They leave out tokenization, task heads, and the cost of training five out-of-fold CafeBERT teachers for each task, which is substantial even though it is paid only once.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GoiBERT is trained on publicly available text and benchmarks, so it can pick up social biases from web data and from its teachers.
We have not audited the models for such biases, and we encourage
anyone deploying them in sensitive settings to check first.
On the positive side, smaller models make Vietnamese NLP cheaper to run and accessible on more modest hardware.</p>
</div>
</section>
